WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.436 --> 00:00:03.431
- We going to either, we going
to either succeed or die tryin'.

00:00:03.431 --> 00:00:04.691
It is what it is.

00:00:04.691 --> 00:00:06.757
You can't control like
where your gifts come from.

00:00:06.757 --> 00:00:07.940
You can only do somethin' about 'em.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:10.357
You can't completely go off numbers to try

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:11.624
to sell something creative.

00:00:11.624 --> 00:00:13.652
You have to come up with other
creative ways to sell it.

00:00:13.652 --> 00:00:15.476
It isn't about what I
have, it's me knowing

00:00:15.476 --> 00:00:17.888
my weaknesses and what I don't have.

00:00:17.888 --> 00:00:18.872
- Hello, Believe Nation.

00:00:18.872 --> 00:00:21.554
It's Evan, my one word
is, believe, and I believe

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:23.898
in people more than they
believe in themselves

00:00:23.898 --> 00:00:26.941
and my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:26.941 --> 00:00:30.514
what I see in you, you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:30.514 --> 00:00:32.423
So, to help you on your journey
today we're going to learn

00:00:32.423 --> 00:00:34.690
from singer and song writer, The Dream,

00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:37.710
and my take on his top
10 rules of success.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.290
Rule number five is my personal favorite

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:41.773
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:41.773 --> 00:00:43.125
Also, as you're watching,
if you hear something

00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:45.293
that really resonates
with you, please leave it

00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:47.001
down in the comments below and put quotes

00:00:47.001 --> 00:00:48.357
around it so other people can be inspired

00:00:48.357 --> 00:00:50.039
by it, as well and if you leave it

00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:51.337
within the first couple of hours

00:00:51.337 --> 00:00:52.827
of this video going
live, you have a chance

00:00:52.827 --> 00:00:55.410
to win one of two daily prizes.

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:01.110
(upbeat music)

00:01:08.385 --> 00:01:10.911
- I took everything that I
learned from a ethics standpoint

00:01:10.911 --> 00:01:14.375
that my grandfather taught
me and put it into that.

00:01:14.375 --> 00:01:18.557
Sayin' if you pound, pound
it as hard at whatever thing

00:01:18.557 --> 00:01:20.970
in the same direction, if you
keep hitting it, at a certain

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.191
point it's going to
break and it'll open up.

00:01:23.191 --> 00:01:24.675
So, that's what I did.

00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:26.359
Like, it was like, okay, cool,
this is what I'm going to do.

00:01:26.359 --> 00:01:27.841
I'm going to focus on this thing.

00:01:27.841 --> 00:01:31.154
We going to either succeed or die tryin'.

00:01:31.154 --> 00:01:32.358
It is what it is.

00:01:32.358 --> 00:01:36.525
I completely seen all the stars
growing up as human beings

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:39.371
and I think that was the first thing.

00:01:39.371 --> 00:01:41.699
As, and the first person that I made human

00:01:41.699 --> 00:01:43.262
to me was Michael Jackson.

00:01:43.262 --> 00:01:46.197
If I would have ever seen
him I knew in my heart

00:01:46.197 --> 00:01:49.247
that I would treat him like a
person that has these talents.

00:01:49.247 --> 00:01:51.166
You can't control like,
where your gifts come from,

00:01:51.166 --> 00:01:54.330
you can only do somethin'
about 'em or you know,

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.629
go through life and try to
make sure that you leave

00:01:56.629 --> 00:01:58.793
an impression on this world with what you

00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:00.258
have on the inside.

00:02:00.258 --> 00:02:04.091
So, whoever was singing
or writing I felt like

00:02:07.213 --> 00:02:09.731
I wanted to be amongst them.

00:02:09.731 --> 00:02:11.953
I didn't want to look up or
appear to them like I wanted

00:02:11.953 --> 00:02:15.580
to be exactly in line with
those people and I still

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.499
see things that same way today.

00:02:17.499 --> 00:02:21.666
I just developed this sense
of not wanting to have to lean

00:02:22.668 --> 00:02:24.564
on somebody to get my job done.

00:02:24.564 --> 00:02:28.345
So, eventually being around
like all these great producers,

00:02:28.345 --> 00:02:31.404
like starting of course with
Tricky's brother, Laney,

00:02:31.404 --> 00:02:33.776
who actually signed me to
my first publishing contract

00:02:33.776 --> 00:02:38.629
after I wrote this record
called, Everything, for B2K.

00:02:38.629 --> 00:02:42.099
I just picked up the drum
machine, start watching

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:45.215
their patterns, like I
understood how to make a record.

00:02:45.215 --> 00:02:46.063
I knew what a hook was.

00:02:46.063 --> 00:02:48.492
I knew what a B Section
was, and you just musically

00:02:48.492 --> 00:02:50.215
tryin' to understand those same things.

00:02:50.215 --> 00:02:52.637
And I grew up in the '80's
where all those melodic records,

00:02:52.637 --> 00:02:54.082
you know, happened.

00:02:54.082 --> 00:02:57.726
So, for me it was like, I
can hear it like a juke box

00:02:57.726 --> 00:02:59.836
in my head, I just needed to get it out.

00:02:59.836 --> 00:03:02.474
So but, finding that
you have good producers

00:03:02.474 --> 00:03:04.637
that know how to, it could be the smallest

00:03:04.637 --> 00:03:05.775
thing like a kick.

00:03:05.775 --> 00:03:08.635
Like, Timberland has this
way of how he makes his kicks

00:03:08.635 --> 00:03:11.665
and his snares like, and they're
on this little slide beat,

00:03:11.665 --> 00:03:14.026
but you have to know what
you doin' when you hear it.

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:17.393
And so, all these things I get
to get in and listen to them

00:03:17.393 --> 00:03:19.599
and pick it up, and still
now, like I'll listen

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:22.514
to somethin' and say, oh,
okay, that's how you doin' it.

00:03:22.514 --> 00:03:26.111
And even new producers,
like, so along the way

00:03:26.111 --> 00:03:28.559
I just picked up that drum
machine and that keyboard

00:03:28.559 --> 00:03:33.001
and said, okay, I need to
know how to make a song 100%,

00:03:33.001 --> 00:03:37.150
period, without using,
yeah, without needing anyone

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:37.983
if it come to that.

00:03:37.983 --> 00:03:39.362
- Just because at the
moment if you want to create

00:03:39.362 --> 00:03:41.454
and you call somebody and
they can't do it that day,

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:42.932
you don't want to get
frustrated and not be able

00:03:42.932 --> 00:03:43.773
to do it yourself.

00:03:43.773 --> 00:03:44.970
- Yeah, so I learned how to do everything.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.780
It was the beat, it was writin' the song

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.698
and recording and engineering myself.

00:03:48.698 --> 00:03:52.089
Oh wow, nobody ever brings that up.

00:03:52.089 --> 00:03:55.107
Yes, I remember writin' that
song and they were sayin'

00:03:55.107 --> 00:03:56.391
she was finished.

00:03:56.391 --> 00:03:58.190
I was in Vegas working on
I think, Love versus Money,

00:03:58.190 --> 00:03:59.958
at the time and they were
sayin' that she was finished

00:03:59.958 --> 00:04:04.321
on her album and I said, not
if you write a great song

00:04:04.321 --> 00:04:05.779
she not, you know.

00:04:05.779 --> 00:04:06.962
And so, we wrote the song.

00:04:06.962 --> 00:04:08.807
I remember goin' back to
Atlanta and Trick called me

00:04:08.807 --> 00:04:10.732
and he was like, you know,
Celine is taking one song

00:04:10.732 --> 00:04:12.671
off and she puttin' this
song on, she loves it.

00:04:12.671 --> 00:04:16.838
I was like, alright well,
you know, get it poppin',

00:04:17.964 --> 00:04:21.979
and then I think the
conversation led into,

00:04:21.979 --> 00:04:23.100
I think she had tried
to cut the vocal like

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.062
a couple of times in her
way and she was being

00:04:25.062 --> 00:04:28.650
vocal produced by somebody
and she just scrapped

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:32.734
everything and said, no, I
want to sing it how he sang it

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:35.250
with that type of vibe, you know.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:39.544
And, that was the biggest
thing coming from Celine,

00:04:39.544 --> 00:04:43.802
'cause you're like, who could tell anybody

00:04:43.802 --> 00:04:46.890
how to sing something with
a voice like how she have.

00:04:46.890 --> 00:04:50.736
But, the fact she recognized
vibe was a whole different

00:04:50.736 --> 00:04:53.289
idea and I try to teach
people that all the time

00:04:53.289 --> 00:04:55.153
and they don't really get
it, 'cause most of the time

00:04:55.153 --> 00:04:56.874
they think it's about
singing, and it's like no,

00:04:56.874 --> 00:04:59.934
it's about a feeling, you know.

00:04:59.934 --> 00:05:02.956
I get to be the creative
person that sits in that room,

00:05:02.956 --> 00:05:06.685
like, but with some type
of rational idea and not

00:05:06.685 --> 00:05:10.389
just straight art, 'cause I
understand even by running

00:05:10.389 --> 00:05:13.489
a business as a song writer,
people have to make money.

00:05:13.489 --> 00:05:14.976
I get that, you know.

00:05:14.976 --> 00:05:18.996
What I try to apply to it
though, is you can't completely

00:05:18.996 --> 00:05:21.980
go off numbers to try to
sell something creative.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.261
You have to come up with other
creative ways to sell it.

00:05:24.261 --> 00:05:26.260
I was working at some collection agency.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.631
I was really bad at it,
I was really bad at it.

00:05:28.631 --> 00:05:31.074
- Sally Mae, hope it
wasn't Sally Mae, man.

00:05:31.074 --> 00:05:33.918
- No, that's too good,
it was worse than that.

00:05:33.918 --> 00:05:35.206
That's too good.

00:05:35.206 --> 00:05:37.003
I remember just calling,
they say, I don't have it.

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:39.476
I'm like, alright, cool.

00:05:39.476 --> 00:05:42.729
- You a G for that, man, ain't my money.

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:43.850
- It was really bad.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:45.772
And, I had just started playing golf

00:05:45.772 --> 00:05:47.772
at that particular time.

00:05:48.949 --> 00:05:51.496
I stopped by the golf course
at like 7:30 in the morning

00:05:51.496 --> 00:05:55.522
and I got on the first tee
at this golf course called,

00:05:55.522 --> 00:05:59.004
Legacy, and I sat there
and I called into my job

00:05:59.004 --> 00:06:02.714
and I quit, because I kind
of figured that if I didn't

00:06:02.714 --> 00:06:07.248
put 100% into music
really, I was never going

00:06:07.248 --> 00:06:10.037
to actually be great enough to stay in.

00:06:10.037 --> 00:06:12.238
And so, I quit my job that
day not knowing how I was

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:14.264
going to pay rent the next
month and at that time

00:06:14.264 --> 00:06:16.639
I didn't have a publishing deal.

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:20.136
And, that song is actually
what got me a publishing deal.

00:06:20.136 --> 00:06:21.283
- Awesome, man.

00:06:21.283 --> 00:06:22.395
So, you believed in yourself.

00:06:22.395 --> 00:06:24.592
- Like I said, you got to be crazy enough

00:06:24.592 --> 00:06:26.322
to believe in yourself.

00:06:26.322 --> 00:06:27.723
- If it was on, up and
comin' artist right now,

00:06:27.723 --> 00:06:28.972
what advice would you give 'em?

00:06:28.972 --> 00:06:30.446
A serious up and comin' artist

00:06:30.446 --> 00:06:34.131
- Oh, well, I would, pray
if you're with a label

00:06:34.131 --> 00:06:36.695
you should probably
just pray that somebody

00:06:36.695 --> 00:06:39.175
believes in you, like that one person

00:06:39.175 --> 00:06:43.222
versus havin' a whole staff, making sure

00:06:43.222 --> 00:06:46.821
you're being yourself
and eventually you know,

00:06:46.821 --> 00:06:51.636
overall, just overcoming
the fears of not succeeding

00:06:51.636 --> 00:06:53.735
in the eyes of what the public idea

00:06:53.735 --> 00:06:55.921
and perception of succeeding is.

00:06:55.921 --> 00:06:58.004
Be yourself at all times.

00:06:59.839 --> 00:07:03.494
Never sell your soul to any machine.

00:07:03.494 --> 00:07:06.588
Okay, if I become a artist
then, I'm going to stay at it

00:07:06.588 --> 00:07:08.311
because I'm going to
always be singing my own.

00:07:08.311 --> 00:07:10.217
It isn't about what I
have, it's me knowing

00:07:10.217 --> 00:07:12.699
my weaknesses and what I don't have.

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:14.366
You can't trick me into what I am good at.

00:07:14.366 --> 00:07:17.052
Nobody going to write
anything on a blog that's

00:07:17.052 --> 00:07:19.849
going to say, Dream sucks at this.

00:07:19.849 --> 00:07:22.221
Yeah, give your life like, that's not.

00:07:22.221 --> 00:07:23.939
If you see me on the basketball court

00:07:23.939 --> 00:07:25.856
then probably my fadeaway ain't there.

00:07:25.856 --> 00:07:29.262
We can say, he can't play basketball.

00:07:29.262 --> 00:07:30.581
You're right.

00:07:30.581 --> 00:07:32.424
He can't write music, you've lost yo mind.

00:07:32.424 --> 00:07:34.036
I can do anything.

00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:37.824
I broke myself as a
artist at the age of 29.

00:07:37.824 --> 00:07:40.570
If that isn't already
hard enough, you know,

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:44.429
to do what I've done in a
10 year period in this span,

00:07:44.429 --> 00:07:46.331
to even be blessed
enough to be sitting here

00:07:46.331 --> 00:07:48.241
in front of you guys talking about this.

00:07:48.241 --> 00:07:50.841
I could wrap it up and
say, oh, cool, goals done

00:07:50.841 --> 00:07:54.567
and just moving one, but
it's too many other things.

00:07:54.567 --> 00:07:57.992
You know, once you movin' from
one thing it's all art to me.

00:07:57.992 --> 00:08:00.292
It's all in a bowl like,
it's just on a plate.

00:08:00.292 --> 00:08:02.984
There's the Ice Cube I
needed to chase down.

00:08:02.984 --> 00:08:05.206
There's a Dr. Dre I need to chase down.

00:08:05.206 --> 00:08:09.897
It's moved on from Lionel
and Diane to now, it's other

00:08:09.897 --> 00:08:13.066
people that I see and say,
oh, they used that same brain

00:08:13.066 --> 00:08:16.028
to get those things and I
can use my brain and actually

00:08:16.028 --> 00:08:18.200
get to the same things that you have.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:22.111
So it's just a everlasting
idea to keep pushing,

00:08:22.111 --> 00:08:24.101
you know, myself.

00:08:24.101 --> 00:08:27.048
This music thing is like
water, like it's not, my songs

00:08:27.048 --> 00:08:28.973
are going to be my songs regardless.

00:08:28.973 --> 00:08:32.480
People get hot and cold and wander off,

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:35.753
but you'll be back and when it's time

00:08:35.753 --> 00:08:39.920
for you to get married or fall
in love again, I'll be here.

00:08:41.892 --> 00:08:45.809
But, this idea of what
to do or what to do next

00:08:46.791 --> 00:08:48.440
is what I've been doin' all along.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:50.352
Like, these are just steps.

00:08:50.352 --> 00:08:54.678
You know, I was already
preparing to be able to retire

00:08:54.678 --> 00:08:58.333
at a very early age and not
meaning retire, you never

00:08:58.333 --> 00:09:01.016
see me again, but oh, cool,
I can put my up and now

00:09:01.016 --> 00:09:05.251
I can do whatever the
want to do, you know.

00:09:05.251 --> 00:09:09.183
But, still use myself
and the things that I see

00:09:09.183 --> 00:09:13.397
and the movies I want
to write and direct and

00:09:13.397 --> 00:09:17.675
bring them to the world
'cause there is nobody has any

00:09:17.675 --> 00:09:21.574
real idea what's in my
mind and heart sitting

00:09:21.574 --> 00:09:24.379
on this stage, you just
know what I've done.

00:09:24.379 --> 00:09:26.642
And, when I didn't have that stuff before,

00:09:26.642 --> 00:09:29.202
you didn't know I could do
that, so we have no idea

00:09:29.202 --> 00:09:31.198
what I can actually do.

00:09:31.198 --> 00:09:33.472
We've seen great people come from nothing,

00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:35.750
so if you have something
that means that you should go

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:38.247
to even higher levels of
achieving what your goals

00:09:38.247 --> 00:09:40.234
and your dreams are.

00:09:40.234 --> 00:09:41.245
I speak for all of us.

00:09:41.245 --> 00:09:42.519
We have no excuses.

00:09:42.519 --> 00:09:45.950
We will go on to be
exactly who we want to be

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.900
in pursuit of the American dream.

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:52.067
In the summer of 1992 my mother
was diagnosed with cancer.

00:09:53.541 --> 00:09:56.407
Didn't know at the time how
serious it was, but that summer

00:09:56.407 --> 00:09:59.963
was already a big transition
for me because they were

00:09:59.963 --> 00:10:02.814
closing my high school
down, which was West Fulton,

00:10:02.814 --> 00:10:05.481
so couldn't really get no worse.

00:10:06.316 --> 00:10:08.596
I never thought that I would lose my mom

00:10:08.596 --> 00:10:10.596
at the end of this year.

00:10:11.568 --> 00:10:14.359
- After his mother passed
away his grandfather

00:10:14.359 --> 00:10:16.283
kept him and raised him.

00:10:16.283 --> 00:10:20.450
He provided him with most
everything he would ask for.

00:10:21.781 --> 00:10:25.732
I remember his grandfather
bought him a set of drums

00:10:25.732 --> 00:10:28.819
and he would be back in
his room playin' the drums

00:10:28.819 --> 00:10:30.569
learnin' about music.

00:10:36.512 --> 00:10:39.356
- Who I am as a father, I
take my responsibilities

00:10:39.356 --> 00:10:41.773
as a father very, very heavy.

00:10:43.285 --> 00:10:46.601
I try to do the best that
I can with what I have.

00:10:46.601 --> 00:10:50.113
I try to make sure that I
can provide a better way

00:10:50.113 --> 00:10:54.098
and provide more choices
and options for my children

00:10:54.098 --> 00:10:56.194
that are going to come behind me.

00:10:56.194 --> 00:10:59.857
You know, life is from
generation to generation,

00:10:59.857 --> 00:11:02.125
building on choices.

00:11:02.125 --> 00:11:05.458
(children making noise)

00:11:12.011 --> 00:11:13.629
What loading means is that it's putting

00:11:13.629 --> 00:11:15.342
it all inside your computer.

00:11:15.342 --> 00:11:17.440
So, you have to wait til it get finished.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:18.390
You see that little blue?

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:19.918
When that blue gets all the way to here

00:11:19.918 --> 00:11:21.380
that means it's finished.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:22.781
- That's like on the TV.

00:11:22.781 --> 00:11:23.774
- Yeah, exactly.

00:11:23.774 --> 00:11:25.730
If I had 10 choices and
10 directions I could go,

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:29.045
then my children should
have 100 and their children

00:11:29.045 --> 00:11:33.352
should have 1000, that's
basically what it is.

00:11:33.352 --> 00:11:35.574
Me, knowing that the sacrifices
I had to make you know,

00:11:35.574 --> 00:11:37.988
with my grandfather working
all day and my mother

00:11:37.988 --> 00:11:42.155
working, in no way deters
me, how to be a great father

00:11:45.899 --> 00:11:50.066
in anyone's book is set in
stone or black and white.

00:11:51.053 --> 00:11:53.409
There is no real way to do it.

00:11:53.409 --> 00:11:56.622
There are only your
gifts, your opportunities

00:11:56.622 --> 00:12:00.372
and your choices to do
the best that you can.

00:12:03.839 --> 00:12:05.277
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:12:05.277 --> 00:12:07.951
I made this video because
you guys asked me to.

00:12:07.951 --> 00:12:08.931
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:12:08.931 --> 00:12:11.035
in a future Top 10,
please check out the link

00:12:11.035 --> 00:12:13.665
down in the description below
and you can cast your vote

00:12:13.665 --> 00:12:15.881
on who we should do next.

00:12:15.881 --> 00:12:18.116
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Bini Alex,

00:12:18.116 --> 00:12:20.526
Bini, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:12:20.526 --> 00:12:22.980
Your One Word, and that
awesome message that you

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:23.817
had to share.

00:12:23.817 --> 00:12:25.861
I'm so glad it's havin'
an impact on your life

00:12:25.861 --> 00:12:27.565
and thank you for sharin' it and puttin'

00:12:27.565 --> 00:12:28.809
it on Twitter, too.

00:12:28.809 --> 00:12:30.504
I really, really appreciate the support.

00:12:30.504 --> 00:12:31.521
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:12:31.521 --> 00:12:32.834
I believe in you.

00:12:32.834 --> 00:12:34.501
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:12:34.501 --> 00:12:36.234
and whatever your one word is.

00:12:36.234 --> 00:12:37.494
Much love.

00:12:37.494 --> 00:12:38.994
I'll see you soon.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:49.415
- By the time we get to about
2007, imagine that you have

00:12:49.415 --> 00:12:51.802
more control of your vision.

00:12:51.802 --> 00:12:53.712
It's really shinin'
through now with a record

00:12:53.712 --> 00:12:55.379
called, I'm Brother.

00:12:56.669 --> 00:13:00.502
- Wow, yeah, that record
was done in a act of,

00:13:02.624 --> 00:13:06.073
I would like to imagine
Mohammad Ali on the ropes

00:13:06.073 --> 00:13:10.543
and just takin' it, just
takin' the pounding.

00:13:10.543 --> 00:13:13.009
Life was, I was out of my publishing deal.

00:13:13.009 --> 00:13:16.606
You don't know when
the stars are aligning,

00:13:16.606 --> 00:13:19.285
it just depends on how, how much you can

00:13:19.285 --> 00:13:21.258
keep your head up through it.

00:13:21.258 --> 00:13:23.331
There could be aligning
and you now know it

00:13:23.331 --> 00:13:24.970
which is most of the time.

00:13:24.970 --> 00:13:26.744
I was checkin' pockets and stuff for like,

00:13:26.744 --> 00:13:28.241
maybe I left a extra 20 over here to put

00:13:28.241 --> 00:13:29.536
some gas in this.

00:13:29.536 --> 00:13:30.941
- Did you still have the
Cadillac with leather seats?

00:13:30.941 --> 00:13:33.358
- I had a Jaguar at the time.

00:13:34.453 --> 00:13:38.620
Don't say, ahh, they tried to
repossess it like nine times.

00:13:39.864 --> 00:13:40.887
I hid that good.

00:13:40.887 --> 00:13:43.267
Every time, park down the
street and walk to yo apartment,

00:13:43.267 --> 00:13:45.850
cover this up with some leaves.

00:13:49.927 --> 00:13:54.094
And, it was just a weird
space to say, write a hit.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:57.508
You know what a hit sounds like.

00:13:57.508 --> 00:13:59.253
You know what songs you
like from other people.

00:13:59.253 --> 00:14:01.342
Like, if you went to
make a playlist, you knew

00:14:01.342 --> 00:14:03.506
what the a hit was and you
know you know how to make it,

00:14:03.506 --> 00:14:04.954
so just write a hit.

00:14:04.954 --> 00:14:08.329
So, I just told myself
that that day and literally

00:14:08.329 --> 00:14:11.891
we're moving stuff around
in the studio, Koo Korell

00:14:11.891 --> 00:14:15.279
who now's like side by side
with Rihanna and records

00:14:15.279 --> 00:14:18.826
a lot of her stuff for her
now, finds a, finds this loop,

00:14:18.826 --> 00:14:20.076
this kick loop.

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:23.674
Trick starts to play this
chord and I kind of walk in

00:14:23.674 --> 00:14:28.579
at that same moment and I hear
it, and I heard about maybe

00:14:28.579 --> 00:14:31.912
eight bars of what, what trick was doing

00:14:33.845 --> 00:14:35.119
and I said, man, turn the mic on.

00:14:35.119 --> 00:14:36.700
They was like, we not ready,
we just started movin'

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:39.138
this around, we got to plug
it up, we got to get it right.

00:14:39.138 --> 00:14:41.907
Okay, cool, turn the mic on.

00:14:41.907 --> 00:14:43.336
Turned the mic on.

00:14:43.336 --> 00:14:45.738
I mean, we had to go back
and change four words,

00:14:45.738 --> 00:14:48.962
but I sung it from the top to the end

00:14:48.962 --> 00:14:53.105
exactly as is how you hear
the song today, right now.

